// InlineManagedPrimitives — a Std→Std pass that expands the FAST PATH of the five hottest
// `__ManagedMemory` element primitives AT THEIR CALL SITES, and leaves the call itself — verbatim, on a
// slow arm — to serve every other case.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY A PASS AND NOT MERELY A CHEAPER RUNTIME BODY.** The runtime bodies are shared, target-neutral
// Std graphs, and a shared body is reached by a CALL: the `__gt_morestack` prologue check, the frame push
// and pop, the argument moves, the `call` and the `ret`. MEASURED 2026-08-25 (EC0's instrument —
// `scripts/self-host-ab.sh` + `scripts/sample_profile.py`): the compiler shv2 EMITS runs the identical
// self-compile **1.8× slower** than the bootstrap-emitted one, and ~40% of stage-2's samples sit in these
// bodies — `__managed_mem_set` 21%, `__managed_get` 10.8%, `__managed_cow_detach` 4.1%, `__managed_set`
// 3.5%. That is ~25 instructions of protocol around a ~10-instruction fast path, paid per ELEMENT ACCESS,
// and **no body can make it cheaper because it is not in the body.** The bootstrap emits the same fast
// paths inline (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/MLIR/Conversion/MaxonToStandardConversion.ManagedMemory.cs`,
// `LowerManagedMemGet` :246-396 / `LowerManagedMemSet` :604-682); this is shv2's answer to the same
// question, one tier down and shared by every backend.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE PASS RE-DERIVES NOTHING. EVERY TEST IT EMITS IS THE RUNTIME'S OWN EXPORTED EMITTER** —
// `emitIndexOutOfRange`, `emitElementIsManaged`, `emitBufferNotOwned`, `emitIsSharedAllocation`,
// `emitSlotAddr`, `emitLoadField`, `emitCmp`, `emitConst`. ⚠ **`emitElementIsManaged` JOINED THAT LIST IN
// REVIEW AND WAS THE ONE GAP IN IT**: the ownership gate — the single predicate whose answer licenses both
// fast arms to skip the runtime — was the one test this file spelled out of primitives instead, in two
// copies, against three more in the runtime. A hand-copied arm is the drifting checklist
// `buildManagedCowDetach`'s header rejects, and it has already been measured wrong once in this exact
// family: R4.4's hand-written `capacity < 0` stood in for `__managed_cow_detach`'s TWO arms, so a
// `setByte` through the owner of a VIEWED buffer wrote straight through the sharing — **198 where the
// answer is 110**
// (`managed-memory-methods.set-byte-through-a-viewed-owner-detaches-first`). What this pass adds is the
// ROUTING, never a predicate.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SLOW ARM IS THE ORIGINAL CALL, MOVED — NOT A RE-IMPLEMENTATION.** Every refusal, every throw,
// every COW detach, every element destructor and the whole packed/narrow-stride world stay exactly where
// they are. The pass never DECIDES anything: it emits a chain of preconditions and, on the first one that
// does not hold, hands control to the untouched call. So the answer this compiler produces is the runtime's
// answer for every input, and the only thing a fast arm can be wrong about is *taking itself when it should
// not have* — which is why each precondition below is the runtime's own emitter and nothing else.
//
// ⭐ **EVERY PRECONDITION IS PURE AND FAULT-FREE, WHICH IS WHY THEIR ORDER IS FREE — WITH ONE EXCEPTION
// THAT IS NOT.** The tests are field loads off a record the caller already holds plus compares, so
// reordering them cannot change what the program does. The exception is `emitIsSharedAllocation`, which
// reads the allocation HEADER below `buffer@0` — an interior pointer has none, and a null buffer has no
// address at all — so it is reached only after `emitBufferNotOwned` and the `buffer != 0` test, in exactly
// `__managed_cow_detach`'s own arm order. That ordering is a REQUIREMENT, not a preference.
//
// ⭐⭐ **SO THE ORDER IS SPENT ON THE ONE THING IT CAN BUY: THE MOST DISCRIMINATING TEST FIRST.** A fast
// arm that ends up taking the call has paid for every guard ahead of the one that refused, ON TOP of the
// call it was always going to make — so a family this pass cannot serve must be shown the door early or
// this rung makes it SLOWER. The stride fork goes first and is the only test that refuses a whole element
// type outright: `element_size@24` is neither a machine word nor a single byte for a 2- or 4-byte element,
// an unset stride and every sub-byte-packed width, and each of those leaves in at most two compares. On the
// STORE path `element_destroy@40 != 0` refuses every managed-element array next, whose occupant is the
// runtime's to destroy. The bound and the three copy-on-write arms, which refuse rarely, follow.
//
// ⚠ **EC1 MEASURED THAT DOOR ON `ByteArray`, AND EC3 MOVED `ByteArray` TO THE OTHER SIDE OF IT.** The
// number this paragraph used to carry — a `ByteArray` element access paying 4 instructions before reaching
// its call rather than 13 — was about the arm that now SERVES it. What still pays it is the narrower set
// above.
//
// ⚠ **WHERE IT IS SCHEDULED, AND WHY IT IS NOT WHERE IT WAS FIRST PLANNED (EC1-b).** It runs AFTER
// `insertRangeChecks` and before `pruneDeadBlockArgs`/`foldConstOperands`. Before the range checks it
// CANNOT go: a range-check site is recorded as `(blockId, stdOpPos)` in the coordinate space
// `lowerMaxonToStd` left the block in (`RangeCheckSite.stdOpPos`, `Project.maxon`), and this pass SPLITS
// blocks and removes an op from one — so every site recorded past a split point would either land in a
// block that no longer holds it or trip `guardSiteAt`'s own `pastTheBlock` panic. Running after costs
// nothing and keeps that coordinate space untouched. Before `foldConstOperands` it must go, and that half
// of the plan stands: every bound and stride this pass emits is a `const`, and the fold is what turns them
// into `cmpImm`/`binOpImm` operands so they never occupy a register in the loops this pass makes hotter.
//
// ⚠ **REGISTER PRESSURE IS THE COST, AND IT IS PAID IN THE LOOPS THE ALLOCATOR REFUSES TO SPILL.** Each
// fast arm holds a handful of values live across its guard chain. They are kept to the minimum that is
// still one spelling: the stride constant is re-minted in the block that uses it rather than carried from
// the block that compared against it (the fold deletes both), and `element_size@24` dies at its own test.
// The values that genuinely span the region are the call's own arguments, which were live across the call
// this replaces.

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO STRIDES THESE FAST ARMS ARE THE WHOLE OF: ONE MACHINE WORD, AND ONE BYTE.**
// `element_size@24` is SIGNED and runtime-valued — a byte stride when positive, `-(bits per element)` when
// the elements are sub-byte packed (`ManagedMemoryRuntime`'s header) — and a target moves exactly two of
// those widths in ONE memory op (`emitStrideDispatch`'s header states the list and why it is those two).
// Every other stride (2, 4, the unset 0, and every packed one) goes to the runtime, whose
// `emitLoadElemAt`/`emitStoreElemAt` own that arithmetic and its zero-extension rules.
//
// ⭐ **THE BYTE ARM JOINED IN EC3, AND IT IS NOT A GENERALIZATION — IT IS A MEASURED FAMILY.** Every
// `ByteArray` access and every 1-byte enum slot (`enum-narrow-storage`) MISSED the word arm and paid the
// whole runtime call: `__managed_get`'s slow arm reached from `Map.findSlot` alone is 2% of a stage-2
// self-compile, and the body it lands in walks an eight-trip byte loop to move one byte.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER WIDTH IS SPELLED IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT** (found in review of EC3-F3). Each
// arm below names a `ManagedMemoryRuntime.SingleOpStride`, which carries the `element_size@24` stamp AND
// the Std memory type an access at that stamp uses as ONE fact — see its header for the wrong answers a
// site able to name the two separately can produce. This file briefly carried its own `WordElementStride`
// derived from `MachineWordBytes`, a second derivation of a width the runtime already derives, paired by
// hand with `StdType.i64` at each of three call sites and with `StdType.u8` at three more.

// The block labels this pass mints. They reach the committed goldens, so they are short, prefixed
// `__im_` (inline-managed, disjoint from `InsertRangeChecks`' `__rc_`), and each names WHAT THE BLOCK IT
// LABELS DOES rather than which guard sent control there.
let InlineContLabel = b"__im_cont"
let InlineSlowLabel = b"__im_slow"
let InlineBoundsLabel = b"__im_bounds"
let InlineLoadLabel = b"__im_load"
let InlineEmptyLabel = b"__im_empty"
let InlineOwnedLabel = b"__im_owned"
let InlineBufferLabel = b"__im_buffer"
let InlineViewedLabel = b"__im_viewed"
let InlineStoreLabel = b"__im_store"
// The stride fork's own three: the block that asks the SECOND of `emitStrideDispatch`'s two questions, and
// the entry of each accepted arm. Named here rather than left to the runtime's `esbyte` default so every
// block this pass puts inside a user function still reads as this pass's.
let InlineStrideLabel = b"__im_stride"
let InlineWordLabel = b"__im_word"
let InlineByteLabel = b"__im_byte"
// Where the two widths of a checked `get` rejoin, carrying the loaded element as a block arg.
let InlineLoadedLabel = b"__im_loaded"

// How many sites the pass expanded — a diagnostic counter, reported once per compile.
typealias InlinedSiteCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// The minter every `PrimitiveInliner` starts with and NONE OF THEM EVER MINTS FROM: `valueIds()` replaces
// it with a real one before the first mint, so this exists only so that a function with no site pays no
// allocation. Shared for the reason `InsertRangeChecks.sharedEmptyGuardChainTail` is shared, and safe for
// the same reason — the one operation that would write to it is the one that replaces it.
let sharedUnseededMinter = ValueMinter.create(0)

// The two blocks every carved site has, held together because every expander wires both.
type InlineFrame
	// Takes the ORIGINAL result ids as its block args and holds every op that followed the call.
	export var cont as IrBlock
	// The `BlockRef` naming that continuation, CAPTURED at the split rather than searched for afterwards.
	// The split is the one moment it is knowable in O(1) — `splitBlockExit` is the only `addBlock` inside
	// it, so the continuation is the function's last-registered block exactly then and never again. A
	// search for it later would be O(blocks) per site, i.e. Θ(sites × blocks) over a function that
	// accesses many elements — which is precisely the function this pass exists for.
	export var contRef as BlockRef
	// Holds the original call, verbatim, defining FRESH result ids.
	export var slow as IrBlock

	export static function create(cont IrBlock, contRef BlockRef, slow IrBlock) returns InlineFrame
		return Self{cont: cont, contRef: contRef, slow: slow}
	end 'create'
end 'InlineFrame'

// Expand every inlinable `__ManagedMemory` primitive call in the module.
export function inlineManagedPrimitives(project Project, module StdModule)
	var expanded = 0 as InlinedSiteCount
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// ⭐ A STDLIB FUNCTION NO PATH FROM `main` REACHES IS SKIPPED — the same reachability fact
		// `insertRangeChecks` and `lowerMaxonToStd` skip on, stated the same way. Here it is purely cost:
		// this pass registers no `.rdata` and so cannot move a golden through `nextStringId` the way those
		// two could, and `eliminateDeadFunctions` drops the function before isel either way. What it buys
		// is not doing per-block work for the large unreachable tail of every listed stdlib module.
		if project.stdlibFacts.unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'

		var inliner = PrimitiveInliner.create(module, func: func)
		inliner.run()
		expanded = expanded + inliner.expanded
	end 'eachFunc'

	let total = expanded
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineManagedPrimitives: expanded {total} managed-primitive call site(s) across {module.funcCount()} function(s)")
end 'inlineManagedPrimitives'

// Per-function expander: owns the module and function it rewrites plus the fresh-ValueId counter its
// inline ops draw from, seeded past every id the function already uses.
type PrimitiveInliner
	var module as StdModule
	var func as IrFunction
	// ⚠ **SEEDED LAZILY, AND THAT IS A COST DECISION.** `nextFreeValueId` walks every block-arg and every
	// live op of the function and MATERIALIZES the live-op index array to do it — and this pass visits
	// EVERY function in the module, the overwhelming majority of which hold no managed primitive at all.
	// Seeded in `create`, each of those would pay a second whole-function walk plus its allocation for
	// nothing. It starts as `sharedUnseededMinter`, which is never minted from, and is REPLACED by a real
	// minter at the first site — the same shared-empty-until-needed shape `InsertRangeChecks` gives its
	// three chain columns, and for the same measured reason.
	var ids as ValueMinter
	var seeded as bool
	export var expanded as InlinedSiteCount

	static function create(module StdModule, func IrFunction) returns PrimitiveInliner
		return Self{module: module, func: func, ids: sharedUnseededMinter, seeded: false, expanded: 0}
	end 'create'

	// The function's fresh-ValueId counter, seeded past every id it already uses on first use.
	function valueIds() returns ValueMinter
		if self.seeded 'already'
			return self.ids
		end 'already'

		self.ids = ValueMinter.create(nextFreeValueId(self.module, func: self.func))
		self.seeded = true
		return self.ids
	end 'valueIds'

	// ⭐ **THE BOUND IS SNAPSHOTTED, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THIS LOOP OFF ITS OWN OUTPUT.** Every block an
	// expansion appends — guard chain, slow arm, continuation — is appended to the very array this loop
	// walks, so the count is taken once and none of them is re-entered from here. The CONTINUATIONS are
	// revisited, by `expandChainFrom`, which is where the next site can only be; see its header for why
	// following only those is what makes the walk terminate.
	function run()
		let originalBlockCount = self.func.blockRefs.count()
		for i in 0 upto originalBlockCount 'eachBlock'
			let blockRef = try self.func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("PrimitiveInliner.run: blockRefs.get OOB at {i} despite the snapshot bound")
			self.expandChainFrom(blockRef)
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'run'

	// ⭐⭐ **WALK ONE ORIGINAL BLOCK AND EVERY CONTINUATION A CARVE LEAVES BEHIND IT, IN ONE LOOP** — a
	// carve moves everything past the site into a continuation, so that continuation is where the next site
	// can only be, and the scan restarts at its first op.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT WALKS `block.opRefs`, WHICH IS THE BODY HALF OF `IrModule.liveOpIndices`' OWN DESCENT — never
	// the flat `module.ops`.** That array is append-only and full of orphans from every earlier pass; and a
	// site needs its POSITION IN ITS BLOCK, which the flat array cannot give. A terminator is never a call,
	// so the half this walk omits holds no site.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT MAKES THE WALK TERMINATE IS *WHICH* NEW BLOCK IT FOLLOWS.** An expansion appends three
	// kinds of block: the GUARD CHAIN (which holds no call at all), the SLOW arm (which holds the very call
	// just expanded) and the CONTINUATION (which holds the rest of the site block's ops, and so may hold the
	// next call). Only the CONTINUATION is ever revisited, so the moved call is never rediscovered and the
	// walk cannot cycle — which is why there is no "already expanded" marker column anywhere in this file,
	// and why `run`'s bound is SNAPSHOTTED: every block this pass appends is appended to the very array that
	// loop is walking. There is no worklist either: a carve hands back exactly ONE block to carry on in, so
	// a chain of carves is this loop rather than a list, and a function with no site allocates nothing here.
	function expandChainFrom(blockRef BlockRef)
		var current = blockRef
		var pos = 0 as BlockOpPos
		while pos < self.blockAt(current).opRefs.count() 'eachOp'
			let next = self.expandAt(current, pos: pos)
			if next == current 'nothingCarved'
				pos = pos + 1
				continue
			end 'nothingCarved'

			current = next
			pos = 0
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'expandChainFrom'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE POSITION — AND THE ANSWER IS THE BLOCK THE SCAN CARRIES ON IN.** For everything that is not
	// an expanded site (which is almost every op in the module) that block is the one it was handed, and the
	// caller simply steps past the op; for a carve it is the CONTINUATION, and the caller restarts at its
	// first op. A carve can never hand back the block it was given: its continuation comes from `addBlock`,
	// which appends past every ref the function already had, so the two are told apart by identity and
	// nothing has to be wrapped to say which happened.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THAT IS AN ALLOCATION DECISION, MEASURED.** This routine is called once per op in the whole
	// module, and a PAYLOAD-BEARING union heap-boxes EVERY case, payload-free ones included (`Parser`'s
	// `enumOf` note). The classification and the outcome used to be two such unions — a
	// `ManagedCallSite.notInlinable` and a `BlockExpansion.noSite` minted and dropped at every op that is
	// neither — which cost **2 allocations and 64 bytes per Std op in the program**: on a straight-line
	// ladder holding ZERO expandable sites the phase read 594 / 1,106 / 2,130 / 4,178 allocations across a
	// doubling program, which is the boxes and nothing else. `BlockRef` is an `int`, so this answer is a
	// register. — MEASURED 2026-08-25, `Testing/ladders/genmanagedsites.sh nosites`.
	//
	// ⚠ The `StdOp` match is EXHAUSTIVE: the two call variants are named and everything else is covered by
	// range arms, so a variant APPENDED past the end of the union fails to compile here until it is
	// consciously placed — the band-append invariant working in the reviewer's favour.
	//
	// ⚠ `tryCall` is ALSO how an EXISTENTIAL-returning call is spelled (its second def is the paired witness
	// table, not an error flag — `LowerMaxonToStd.appendDirectCall`). Dispatching on the callee NAME is what
	// keeps the two apart: no `__managed_*` entry returns an interface.
	function expandAt(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns BlockRef
		match self.opAt(blockRef, pos: pos) 'op'
			const to param then break 'op'
			call(resultId, callee, args) then return self.expandPlainCall(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId)
			retVoid to rdataAddr then break 'op'
			tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, callee, args) then return self.expandThrowingCall(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId)
			// ⚠ Extended past `osThreadCpuTicks` when the allocator rung appended seven ops (memFill, the
			// reserve/commit/decommit family, and the atomics). None is a CALL, so none can be a managed
			// primitive to expand — this pass dispatches on a callee NAME and they have none. Placed
			// consciously, which is what the band-append invariant asked for.
			errorReturn to osSetEvent then break 'op'
		end 'op'

		return blockRef
	end 'expandAt'

	// The two NON-throwing primitives. A `call` to anything else, `__managed_*` entries this pass does not
	// expand included, leaves the scan where it was. What each expansion IS, and why, is at the expander
	// it names.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CALLEE'S NAME IS WHAT SURVIVES THE LOWERING, WHICH IS WHY BOTH ROSTERS DISPATCH ON IT.** A
	// runtime callee reaches Std as a plain `ByteArray` on `call`/`tryCall` (`LowerMaxonToStd`'s
	// `lowerRuntimeCall` / `appendThrowingCall`) — there is no tag left to match. The names compared
	// against are the runtime's OWN exported constants and never literals, so this roster and the builders
	// that emit those entries are one fact rather than two.
	function expandPlainCall(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId) returns BlockRef
		if callee.equals(ManagedCountName) 'count'
			return self.rewriteCountInPlace(blockRef, pos: pos, args: args, resultId: resultId, callee: callee)
		end 'count'
		if callee.equals(ManagedGetUncheckedName) 'uncheckedGet'
			return self.expandUncheckedGet(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId)
		end 'uncheckedGet'

		return blockRef
	end 'expandPlainCall'

	// The three THROWING ones, reached as an `StdOp.tryCall` over the dual-register `(value, flag)` ABI and
	// told apart the same way.
	//
	// ⭐ `set` and `mem_set` differ in exactly ONE thing — the record field their index is bounded against —
	// so they share an expander and the difference is this dispatch's `limitOffset` argument and nothing else.
	// `guardIndexInRange` is where that field's two answers are stated; ⚖ the capacity bound is the user
	// ruling of 2026-07-30, carried by `ManagedMemSetName`.
	function expandThrowingCall(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId) returns BlockRef
		if callee.equals(ManagedGetName) 'checkedGet'
			return self.expandCheckedGet(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId)
		end 'checkedGet'
		if callee.equals(ManagedSetName) 'set'
			return self.expandElementStore(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, limitOffset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset)
		end 'set'
		if callee.equals(ManagedMemSetName) 'memSet'
			return self.expandElementStore(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, limitOffset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset)
		end 'memSet'

		return blockRef
	end 'expandThrowingCall'

	// ⭐ `__managed_count(arr)` IS THE ONE MEMBER WITH NOTHING TO GUARD: its whole body is
	// `loadIndirect i64 [arr + length@8]` (`buildManagedCount`), which is legal wherever the call was —
	// the record is the caller's own and no field of it is consulted. So the call op is REPLACED at its own
	// index, keeping `resultId`, and not one block or edge moves. `IrModule.ops` is indexed by
	// `block.opRefs`, so an in-place replacement is invisible to every other reference in the function —
	// the same discipline the register allocator's colorer states for itself.
	function rewriteCountInPlace(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, callee ByteArray) returns BlockRef
		let arr = argAt(args, index: 0, callee: callee)
		let opIdx = self.opIndexAt(blockRef, pos: pos)
		try self.module.ops.set(opIdx, value: StdOp.loadIndirect(resultId, addrId: arr, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64)) otherwise panic("rewriteCountInPlace: module.ops.set OOB at {opIdx}, which block {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' names at position {pos}")
		self.expanded = self.expanded + 1

		return blockRef
	end 'rewriteCountInPlace'

	// `__managed_get_unchecked(arr, index) -> value`: each accepted stride is a slot address and one load, at
	// its own width. There is nothing above the widths to share, so the stride fork IS the whole guard chain
	// and neither arm costs the other anything.
	// There is deliberately NO bounds check and NO empty-slot check here — not because they are cheap to
	// omit, but because `__managed_get_unchecked`'s body does not have them either: the `for`-in header
	// re-reads the length live on every trip and a managed array cannot hold an empty slot inside
	// `[0, count)` (`ManagedGetUncheckedName`'s header states both, and names the parser rule the second
	// rests on). Adding one here would be this pass deciding something, which it does not do.
	function expandUncheckedGet(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId) returns BlockRef
		let arr = argAt(args, index: 0, callee: callee)
		let index = argAt(args, index: 1, callee: callee)
		let frame = self.openPlain(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId)

		var wordArm = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineWordLabel)
		var byteArm = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineByteLabel)
		self.dispatchOnStride(self.blockAt(blockRef), arr: arr, wordArm: wordArm.id, byteArm: byteArm.id, slow: frame.slow.id)
		self.emitLoadArmToCont(wordArm, arr: arr, index: index, stride: SingleOpStride.word, cont: frame.cont)
		self.emitLoadArmToCont(byteArm, arr: arr, index: index, stride: SingleOpStride.byte, cont: frame.cont)

		return self.finish(frame)
	end 'expandUncheckedGet'

	// One width's whole answer for the unchecked get: the slot load, then the continuation edge carrying it.
	function emitLoadArmToCont(cur IrBlock, arr ValueId, index ValueId, stride SingleOpStride, cont IrBlock)
		let value = self.emitElementLoad(cur, arr: arr, index: index, stride: stride)
		self.module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
		attachEdge(cur, targetId: cont.id, argIds: argsOf(value))
	end 'emitLoadArmToCont'

	// `__managed_get(arr, index) -> (value, flag)`. The fast arm is `buildManagedGet`'s success path with
	// its two branches kept and its byte loop collapsed: a single-op stride, in range, and — for a MANAGED
	// element only — a slot that is not empty.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE EMPTY-SLOT ARM IS GATED ON THE DESTRUCTOR, EXACTLY AS `emitLoadElemAndOkReturn` GATES IT.** A
	// trivial element's 0 is a genuine value and must not throw; a managed element's NULL is a slot
	// `resize` grew and nothing wrote, and reading its fields would fault, so it throws `emptySlot`. Only
	// that second case leaves for the slow arm, which is where the throw lives — so an array of Strings
	// keeps the inline read for every slot that holds one.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE BOUND IS PER-ARM AND THE EMPTY-SLOT TAIL IS SHARED, WHICH IS WHERE THE WIDTHS DIVERGE AND
	// WHERE THEY DO NOT.** The bound is three ops, so emitting it once per width costs less than keeping
	// `element_size@24` live across it to re-ask which width this is; the tail is six and reads only the
	// loaded VALUE, so the two arms rejoin at `loaded` carrying it as a block arg and the tail is written —
	// and emitted — once.
	function expandCheckedGet(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId) returns BlockRef
		let arr = argAt(args, index: 0, callee: callee)
		let index = argAt(args, index: 1, callee: callee)
		let frame = self.openThrowing(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId)

		var wordArm = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineWordLabel)
		var byteArm = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineByteLabel)
		self.dispatchOnStride(self.blockAt(blockRef), arr: arr, wordArm: wordArm.id, byteArm: byteArm.id, slow: frame.slow.id)

		var loaded = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineLoadedLabel)
		let value = self.valueIds().mint()
		// ⚠ **THE `loaded` JOIN'S ARG IS AN ELEMENT AND NOT A CALL RESULT, AND IT IS i64 FOR A DIFFERENT
		// REASON FROM THE ONE BELOW (EC3).** The byte arm reaches it with a `u8` load, which is a
		// ZERO-EXTENDED byte in a general register — the same register file and the same machine word, so
		// i64 is its class too. The only backend that distinguishes them is wasm, where a byte access is
		// i32-backed and the phi copy COERCES at the edge (`StdToWasm.emitLocalCopy`) — which is exactly
		// what the runtime's own `emitLoadElemAt` has relied on for its byte arm since EC1.
		attachBlockArgs(loaded, valueIds: argsOf(value), argType: StdType.i64)

		self.emitBoundedLoadArm(wordArm, arr: arr, index: index, stride: SingleOpStride.word, slow: frame.slow, loaded: loaded)
		self.emitBoundedLoadArm(byteArm, arr: arr, index: index, stride: SingleOpStride.byte, slow: frame.slow, loaded: loaded)

		// ⚠ THREE CONSTANTS THAT ARE ALL ZERO TODAY, AND THEY STAY THREE. "no error in flight", "no
		// per-element destructor" (inside `emitElementIsManaged`, which owns that one) and "an empty managed
		// slot" are three different facts that happen to share a value; folding them into one `const 0` would
		// make a change to any one of them silently rewrite the other two — the argument
		// `MemoryIndexOutOfBoundsOrdinal` makes about not aliasing itself to `ArrayErrorIndexOutOfBoundsFlag`.
		// It costs nothing: `foldConstOperands` folds both compares into immediates and DCEs their consts,
		// leaving only the flag, whose reader is the edge below.
		let noError = emitConst(self.module, block: loaded, value: NoErrorFlag, ids: self.valueIds())
		let destroy = emitLoadField(self.module, block: loaded, record: arr, offset: ManagedRecordElementDestroyOffset, ids: self.valueIds())
		let managed = emitElementIsManaged(self.module, block: loaded, destroy: destroy, ids: self.valueIds())

		var checkEmpty = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineEmptyLabel)
		self.module.setTerminator(loaded, op: StdOp.condBranch(managed, thenBlockId: checkEmpty.id, elseBlockId: frame.cont.id))
		attachEdge(loaded, targetId: frame.cont.id, argIds: twoArgs(value, second: noError))

		// `value` is `loaded`'s own block arg and `noError` is defined there, and `loaded` dominates this
		// block, so both are readable here and on this block's own edge to the continuation.
		let emptySlot = emitConst(self.module, block: checkEmpty, value: EmptyManagedSlot, ids: self.valueIds())
		let isEmpty = emitCmp(self.module, block: checkEmpty, a: value, b: emptySlot, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: self.valueIds())
		self.module.setTerminator(checkEmpty, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEmpty, thenBlockId: frame.slow.id, elseBlockId: frame.cont.id))
		attachEdge(checkEmpty, targetId: frame.cont.id, argIds: twoArgs(value, second: noError))

		return self.finish(frame)
	end 'expandCheckedGet'

	// One width's half of the checked get: the `length@8` bound, then the slot load, then the edge into the
	// shared tail carrying what it read.
	function emitBoundedLoadArm(cur IrBlock, arr ValueId, index ValueId, stride SingleOpStride, slow IrBlock, loaded IrBlock)
		var at = self.guardIndexInRange(cur, arr: arr, index: index, limitOffset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, slow: slow, next: InlineLoadLabel)
		let value = self.emitElementLoad(at, arr: arr, index: index, stride: stride)
		self.module.setTerminator(at, op: StdOp.branch(loaded.id))
		attachEdge(at, targetId: loaded.id, argIds: argsOf(value))
	end 'emitBoundedLoadArm'

	// `__managed_set` / `__managed_mem_set`. ⭐⭐ **THE COW ARMS ARE `__managed_cow_detach`'s OWN THREE
	// ARMS, ASKED IN ITS OWN ORDER, AND ANSWERED BY REFUSING RATHER THAN BY DETACHING.** A detach that has
	// nothing to do is the case a write in a loop takes every iteration; a detach that has something to do
	// allocates and copies, and that belongs in the runtime. So this arm proves the three conditions under
	// which the detach would have returned without touching anything — the buffer is this record's
	// (`emitBufferNotOwned`), it exists, and nobody is viewing it (`emitIsSharedAllocation`) — and hands
	// every other shape to the call, which detaches exactly as it always did.
	//
	// ⚠ The order of those three is NOT free: `emitIsSharedAllocation` reads the allocation header below
	// `buffer@0`, which a view's interior pointer does not have and a null buffer does not have either.
	// This is `buildManagedCowDetach`'s arm order for that reason, not by imitation.
	//
	// ⚠ **A MANAGED ELEMENT LEAVES FOR THE SLOW ARM AT `element_destroy@40`, AND THAT IS AN OWNERSHIP
	// OBLIGATION, NOT A CONSERVATISM.** A `set` over a managed element DESTROYS the occupant it overwrites
	// (`emitElementStoreAndOkReturn`), and the throwing path DESTROYS the element it refused to store
	// (`emitDestroyRejectedElement`). Both are the runtime's, both are gated on this field, and a fast arm
	// that stored over an occupant would be a leak — measured at exit 101 when that call was missing from
	// `__managed_set` itself (R4.6).
	function expandElementStore(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId, limitOffset ByteOffset) returns BlockRef
		let arr = argAt(args, index: 0, callee: callee)
		let index = argAt(args, index: 1, callee: callee)
		let value = argAt(args, index: 2, callee: callee)
		let frame = self.openThrowing(blockRef, pos: pos, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId)

		var wordArm = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineWordLabel)
		var byteArm = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineByteLabel)
		self.dispatchOnStride(self.blockAt(blockRef), arr: arr, wordArm: wordArm.id, byteArm: byteArm.id, slow: frame.slow.id)
		self.emitStoreArm(wordArm, arr: arr, index: index, value: value, limitOffset: limitOffset, stride: SingleOpStride.word, frame: frame)
		self.emitStoreArm(byteArm, arr: arr, index: index, value: value, limitOffset: limitOffset, stride: SingleOpStride.byte, frame: frame)

		return self.finish(frame)
	end 'expandElementStore'

	// ⭐ **ONE WIDTH'S WHOLE FAST ARM, AND THE STORE'S FIVE GUARDS LIVE HERE RATHER THAN ABOVE THE STRIDE
	// FORK.** Every one of them is width-INDEPENDENT, so they could equally have been asked once above a
	// second stride test placed just before the store — and that shape was rejected: it puts two more
	// instructions (a compare and a branch) on the WORD path, which is every `Array with Int`, `String`
	// element and pointer slot in the language, to save emitting five guards a second time. The guards are
	// paid in CODE SIZE, once per call site; the second test would be paid in TIME, once per element access.
	// Nothing about the arms differs but the stride constant and the access width, which is why they are one
	// function called twice rather than two.
	function emitStoreArm(cur IrBlock, arr ValueId, index ValueId, value ValueId, limitOffset ByteOffset, stride SingleOpStride, frame InlineFrame)
		var at = cur

		let destroy = emitLoadField(self.module, block: at, record: arr, offset: ManagedRecordElementDestroyOffset, ids: self.valueIds())
		let managed = emitElementIsManaged(self.module, block: at, destroy: destroy, ids: self.valueIds())
		at = self.guard(at, refused: managed, slow: frame.slow, next: InlineBoundsLabel)

		at = self.guardIndexInRange(at, arr: arr, index: index, limitOffset: limitOffset, slow: frame.slow, next: InlineOwnedLabel)

		let notOwned = emitBufferNotOwned(self.module, block: at, record: arr, ids: self.valueIds())
		at = self.guard(at, refused: notOwned, slow: frame.slow, next: InlineBufferLabel)

		let buffer = emitLoadField(self.module, block: at, record: arr, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: self.valueIds())
		let zeroBuf = emitConst(self.module, block: at, value: NoBufferAllocated, ids: self.valueIds())
		let unallocated = emitCmp(self.module, block: at, a: buffer, b: zeroBuf, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: self.valueIds())
		at = self.guard(at, refused: unallocated, slow: frame.slow, next: InlineViewedLabel)

		let viewed = emitIsSharedAllocation(self.module, block: at, ptr: buffer, ids: self.valueIds())
		at = self.guard(at, refused: viewed, slow: frame.slow, next: InlineStoreLabel)

		let strideValue = emitConst(self.module, block: at, value: singleOpStrideBytes(stride), ids: self.valueIds())
		let slot = emitSlotAddr(self.module, block: at, buffer: buffer, index: index, elementSize: strideValue, ids: self.valueIds())
		self.module.appendOp(at, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(slot, offset: 0, valueId: value, valueType: singleOpStrideAccessType(stride)))

		// The ok exit of a throwing VOID entry: the placeholder value the ABI never moves, and the flag,
		// are both `NoErrorFlag` — one `const`, exactly as `emitVoidOkReturn` spells it in the runtime.
		let noError = emitConst(self.module, block: at, value: NoErrorFlag, ids: self.valueIds())
		self.module.setTerminator(at, op: StdOp.branch(frame.cont.id))
		attachEdge(at, targetId: frame.cont.id, argIds: twoArgs(noError, second: noError))
	end 'emitStoreArm'

	// --- the shared pieces every carved expansion is built from ---

	// ⭐ THE ONE PRECONDITION EVERY FAST ARM SHARES — the element is one whole machine word or one byte, so
	// the access is a single `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` at that width. Every other stride leaves for the
	// slow arm HERE, first, which is what keeps a family this pass cannot serve from paying for guards that
	// were never going to admit it.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE TWO COMPARES ARE `emitStrideDispatch`'s, NOT THIS FILE'S.** The list of strides a target
	// moves in one memory op is the runtime's — it is the same list `emitLoadElemAt`/`emitStoreElemAt` route
	// their own fast arms on — and a pass that re-spelled it here would be the drifting checklist this
	// file's header rejects, one predicate further out than the one review already caught
	// (`emitElementIsManaged`). If the tier ever moves a 2- or 4-byte element in one op, that fact changes
	// in one place and both tiers learn it.
	//
	// The caller MINTS the two arm blocks and hands their ids in, rather than taking them back from here:
	// it goes on emitting into both, and a block a caller emits into is a `var` binding of the caller's
	// under this compiler's own ownership rules.
	function dispatchOnStride(cur IrBlock, arr ValueId, wordArm BlockId, byteArm BlockId, slow BlockId)
		let elementSize = emitLoadField(self.module, block: cur, record: arr, offset: ManagedRecordElementSizeOffset, ids: self.valueIds())
		emitStrideDispatch(self.module, func: self.func, cur: cur, es: elementSize, wordArm: wordArm, byteArm: byteArm, otherArm: slow, ids: self.valueIds(), checkByteLabel: InlineStrideLabel)
	end 'dispatchOnStride'

	// The index bound, through `emitIndexOutOfRange` — the ONE home of "reject this index", which carries
	// the NEGATIVE half with it (`StdCmpPred` is signed only, so `-1 >= 3` is false and a negative index
	// would otherwise address before the buffer). The three entries differ only in the record field the
	// limit is read from: `length@8` for the `Array` surface's `get`/`set`, `capacity@16` for the buffer's
	// `mem_set`.
	//
	// ⚠ `__managed_mem_set`'s runtime asks the same question as two separate tests (`emitIsNegative`, then
	// `index >= capacity` inside `emitBufferAccessGuard`) rather than through this composition. The
	// predicate is identical — a disjunction of the same two compares over the same two words — and asking
	// it once here is the composition that already exists rather than a fourth spelling of it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NON-OWNED RECORD LEAVES FOR THE SLOW ARM HERE, NOT AT `emitBufferNotOwned` ONE GUARD LATER, AND
	// THIS LINE CLAIMED THE OPPOSITE UNTIL EC1's REVIEW.** Such a record carries a NEGATIVE `capacity@16`
	// (the `BufferOwnership` encoding), and `index >= capacity` is therefore TRUE for every non-negative
	// index while a negative index is caught by the other disjunct — so on the `mem_set` bound this test
	// already refuses it, unconditionally. Nothing rests on which of the two refuses first (both go to the
	// same slow arm, and the runtime detaches before it reads a capacity for real), but a reader deriving
	// the ordering from the old sentence would be deriving it from something false. The `set` bound is
	// `length@8`, which a view carries honestly, so THERE the refusal really is `emitBufferNotOwned`'s.
	function guardIndexInRange(cur IrBlock, arr ValueId, index ValueId, limitOffset ByteOffset, slow IrBlock, next ByteArray) returns IrBlock
		let limit = emitLoadField(self.module, block: cur, record: arr, offset: limitOffset, ids: self.valueIds())
		let outOfRange = emitIndexOutOfRange(self.module, block: cur, index: index, length: limit, ids: self.valueIds())
		return self.guard(cur, refused: outOfRange, slow: slow, next: next)
	end 'guardIndexInRange'

	// One fast-path precondition: when `refused` holds, control leaves for the slow arm; otherwise it falls
	// into a fresh block the caller goes on emitting into. `cur` is TERMINATED here, so every body op the
	// caller wanted in it must already be there.
	function guard(cur IrBlock, refused ValueId, slow IrBlock, next ByteArray) returns IrBlock
		let ok = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: next)
		self.module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(refused, thenBlockId: slow.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))
		return ok
	end 'guard'

	// The single-op element read: `[buffer@0 + index · stride]` at the arm's own width. The address is
	// `emitSlotAddr`'s — the runtime's one home of "where does element `index` start" — and the load is a
	// plain `loadIndirect` rather than `emitLoadElemAt`, because collapsing that byte loop IS this arm.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TWO AGREE BY CONSTRUCTION AT BOTH STRIDES, AND THE SECOND HALF OF THAT IS A RULE RATHER THAN
	// AN IDENTITY.** At `es == 8` `emitLoadElemAt` accumulates the eight little-endian bytes and masks with
	// `AllBitsSet`, which is the identity for a 64-bit field. At `es == 1` it loads one `u8` — ZERO-extended
	// (`movzx` on x64, `ldrb` on arm64, `i32.load8_u` on wasm) — and masks with `0xFF`, which is that same
	// zero-extended byte; the zero-extension is what `SignatureIndex.arrayElementSize` and
	// `LayoutDescriptor.rangedAliasPackedBits`' `low == 0` rule both rest on, and the parser re-signs a
	// narrow slot from there. So a `u8` load is precisely what the runtime body itself would have answered.
	function emitElementLoad(cur IrBlock, arr ValueId, index ValueId, stride SingleOpStride) returns ValueId
		let buffer = emitLoadField(self.module, block: cur, record: arr, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: self.valueIds())
		let strideValue = emitConst(self.module, block: cur, value: singleOpStrideBytes(stride), ids: self.valueIds())
		let slot = emitSlotAddr(self.module, block: cur, buffer: buffer, index: index, elementSize: strideValue, ids: self.valueIds())
		let value = self.valueIds().mint()
		self.module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(value, addrId: slot, offset: 0, valueType: singleOpStrideAccessType(stride)))
		return value
	end 'emitElementLoad'

	// Carve the call out of its block and build the frame around it: `head` keeps everything BEFORE the
	// call and loses its exit, the CONTINUATION takes everything after it plus that exit, and the call is
	// re-emitted verbatim into a SLOW block — with FRESH result ids, because the ids it used to define are
	// now the continuation's block args, and a value may be defined once.
	//
	// ⚠ The original op is left behind as an ORPHAN in `module.ops`. That is the tier's own model
	// (`IrModule.ops` is append-only and retains what block-rebuilding passes replace), and it is why every
	// reader asks `liveOpIndices` rather than scanning the array.
	function openPlain(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId) returns InlineFrame
		var frame = self.carveFrame(blockRef, pos: pos)
		let slowResult = self.valueIds().mint()
		self.module.appendOp(frame.slow, op: StdOp.call(slowResult, callee: callee, args: args))
		self.closeFrame(frame, carried: argsOf(resultId), slowCarried: argsOf(slowResult))

		return frame
	end 'openPlain'

	// `openPlain`'s dual-register twin: a `tryCall` defines a VALUE and an ERROR FLAG, so both are minted
	// fresh on the slow arm and both become continuation block args. It differs from its twin in exactly
	// that — the op and the arity — which is why the carve and the wiring either side of it are shared.
	function openThrowing(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId) returns InlineFrame
		var frame = self.carveFrame(blockRef, pos: pos)
		let slowResult = self.valueIds().mint()
		let slowFlag = self.valueIds().mint()
		self.module.appendOp(frame.slow, op: StdOp.tryCall(slowResult, errorFlagId: slowFlag, callee: callee, args: args))
		self.closeFrame(frame, carried: twoArgs(resultId, second: errorFlagId), slowCarried: twoArgs(slowResult, second: slowFlag))

		return frame
	end 'openThrowing'

	// The half of an opener that runs BEFORE the call is re-emitted: carve the site block and mint the
	// block the moved call will live in. The returned frame's `slow` is EMPTY and un-terminated — the
	// caller's next act is to append the call into it, which is the one thing the two openers do
	// differently.
	function carveFrame(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns InlineFrame
		// ⛔⛔ **THE MINTER IS SEEDED HERE, BEFORE THE CARVE, AND THAT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING (EC1 review).**
		// `nextFreeValueId` derives its floor from the function's LIVE ops (`IrModule.liveOpIndices`) and its
		// block args — and the carve on the next line makes the call an ORPHAN, out of every `opRefs`, while
		// the ids it defines do not become block args until `closeFrame` several lines later. For that whole
		// gap the function's live set does not contain `resultId`/`errorFlagId`. Seeded inside it, a function
		// whose managed call minted the HIGHEST ids in the function — `try xs.set(…) otherwise return` as the
		// last statement of a void body mints nothing after the `tryCall` — hands the minter a floor at or
		// below `resultId`, and the slow arm's fresh results come back as the very ids the continuation is
		// about to take as block args: TWO DEFINITIONS OF ONE VALUE, which this tier does not refuse, no
		// golden reads back, and the allocator would resolve by whatever its liveness happened to compute.
		// Seeding first is the same one walk at the same one site per function, on a function that still
		// holds the call. See `PrimitiveInliner.ids` for why the seeding is lazy at all.
		//
		// ⚠ **NOT OBSERVED, AND THAT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** A temporary probe comparing the
		// pre- and post-carve floors ran over the shv2 SELF-COMPILE and 1,423 spec cases (`array`,
		// `managed-memory`, `list`, `for-`, `vector`, `slice`) and fired ZERO times, and no hand-built
		// program reached it either — every `tryCall`'s reconciliation mints ids after the call. So this is
		// a coupling made explicit, not a defect repaired: the hazard is that NOTHING states the seeding
		// depends on the call still being live, and the carve two lines down is what ends that.
		_ = self.valueIds()

		let cont = self.carve(blockRef, pos: pos)
		// Read immediately, and CHECKED by identity — `carve` is the one moment the continuation is the
		// function's last-registered block, and the `addBlock` on the next line ends it. The check is not
		// belt-and-braces here: `expandAt` answers "the scan carries on in THIS block" vs "… in the
		// continuation" by COMPARING the two refs, so a reordering that slipped an `addBlock` between the
		// split and this read would hand the walk a guard or slow block — which holds the very call just
		// expanded, and would be rediscovered forever. See `registeredBlockRef` for the rule itself.
		let contRef = registeredBlockRef(self.module, func: self.func, block: cont)

		return InlineFrame.create(cont, contRef: contRef, slow: self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineSlowLabel))
	end 'carveFrame'

	// The half that runs AFTER it: the slow arm branches to the continuation carrying its own fresh
	// results, and the continuation takes the ORIGINAL ones as its block args. That pairing is the whole
	// reason no op after the site has to change — every one of them still names the value it always did.
	function closeFrame(frame InlineFrame, carried ValueIdArray, slowCarried ValueIdArray)
		var slow = frame.slow
		var cont = frame.cont
		self.module.setTerminator(slow, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
		attachEdge(slow, targetId: cont.id, argIds: slowCarried)
		// Every value these five primitives carry is a MACHINE WORD, and that is exact rather than a
		// default: all five are declared `value(StdType.i64)` or void, so the `resultId` a call defined was
		// already classed off that declaration (`StdLoweringShared.calleeResultClass`), and a dual-register
		// error flag is i64 on every backend (`StdToWasm.recordTryCallDefs`).
		attachBlockArgs(cont, valueIds: carried, argType: StdType.i64)
	end 'closeFrame'

	// Split the site block just PAST the call, then drop the call off the head — so the head holds the ops
	// before it, the continuation holds the ops after it, and the call itself is held by neither.
	//
	// It reuses `InsertRangeChecks`' splitter rather than adding a second one: what a split has to get right
	// is moving the block's whole EXIT (terminator, conditional branch and outgoing edge args) onto the
	// continuation while leaving the head un-terminated, and that is one rule with one home.
	function carve(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns IrBlock
		var head = self.blockAt(blockRef)
		let cont = splitBlockInPlace(self.module, func: self.func, head: head, position: pos + 1, label: InlineContLabel)
		head.opRefs.truncate(pos)
		return cont
	end 'carve'

	// The outcome every carved expansion returns, plus the one counter bump they share.
	function finish(frame InlineFrame) returns BlockRef
		self.expanded = self.expanded + 1
		return frame.contRef
	end 'finish'

	// --- block/op access ---

	function blockAt(blockRef BlockRef) returns IrBlock
		return try self.module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("PrimitiveInliner: blockRef {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
	end 'blockAt'

	function opIndexAt(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns OpIndex
		return try self.blockAt(blockRef).opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("PrimitiveInliner: block {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' has no op at position {pos}")
	end 'opIndexAt'

	function opAt(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns StdOp
		let opIdx = self.opIndexAt(blockRef, pos: pos)
		return try self.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("PrimitiveInliner: op index {opIdx} named by block {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is not in module.ops")
	end 'opAt'

end 'PrimitiveInliner'


// The argument at `index`, with the callee named in the failure — a runtime entry's arity is fixed by the
// builder that emitted it, so a mismatch here is a compiler bug and not a program error.
function argAt(args ValueIdArray, index ParamCount, callee ByteArray) returns ValueId
	return try args.get(index) otherwise panic("InlineManagedPrimitives: '{String.from(callee)}' was called with {args.count()} argument(s) and this expansion reads argument {index}")
end 'argAt'

// The value `buffer@0` holds when a record has no element buffer at all — an array that never grew. It is
// the zero `__mm_alloc` leaves the record at, and `__managed_cow_detach`'s `checkShared` arm compares
// against exactly it.
let NoBufferAllocated = 0 as ParsedInt

// An EMPTY managed slot — a slot inside `[0, length)` that a `resize` grew and nothing ever wrote. It is
// zero because `__mm_alloc` zeroes and because every vacating operation erases on the way out (the
// `[length, capacity)`-reads-ZERO invariant `ManagedMemoryRuntime` leans on), and it is what
// `emitLoadElemAndOkReturn`'s null arm compares an element against before throwing `emptySlot`.
//
// ⚠ It is a MANAGED element's emptiness and nothing else's: a TRIVIAL element's 0 is a genuine value, which
// is why the test below it is reached only past the `element_destroy@40` gate.
let EmptyManagedSlot = 0 as ParsedInt
